selector_to_html = {"a[href=\"#term-direct-sum-of-vector-spaces\"]": "<dt id=\"term-direct-sum-of-vector-spaces\">direct sum of vector spaces</dt><dd><p>A way to combine vector spaces while keeping their contributions\nindependent. A vector in <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(V \\oplus W\\)</span> is a pair <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\((v,w)\\)</span>,\nwith <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(v\\in V\\)</span> and <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(w\\in W\\)</span>; addition and scalar\nmultiplication act on each coordinate separately. For example,\n<span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(K^2\\oplus K^3\\)</span> is isomorphic to <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(K^5\\)</span>. For an infinite\ncollection of spaces, only finitely many coordinates of each vector\nmay be nonzero.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#term-preimage\"]": "<dt id=\"term-preimage\">preimage</dt><dd><p>For a map <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(f:X\\to Y\\)</span>, a preimage of an element <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(y\\in Y\\)</span>\nis an element <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(x\\in X\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(f(x)=y\\)</span>. There may be\nno such element, one, or several. For example, under\n<span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}\\)</span>, <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(f(x)=x^2\\)</span>, both\n<span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(2\\)</span> and <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(-2\\)</span> are preimages of <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(4\\)</span>.</p><p>The notation <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(f^{-1}(\\{y\\})\\)</span> denotes the set of all preimages\nof <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(y\\)</span>; it does not require <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(f\\)</span> to have an inverse\nfunction. More generally, the preimage of a subset <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(B\\subseteq Y\\)</span>\nis <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(f^{-1}(B)=\\{x\\in X\\mid f(x)\\in B\\}\\)</span>.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#term-freely-generated-vector-space\"]": "<dt id=\"term-freely-generated-vector-space\">freely generated vector space</dt><dd><p>Think of a set <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(S\\)</span> as an alphabet. Just as letters are building\nblocks for words, the elements of <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(S\\)</span> supply the building\nblocks for vectors. Here we combine them using addition and scalar\nmultiplication. Unlike letters in a word, their order in a sum does\nnot matter, and repeated letters combine: for example,\n<span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(e_a+e_b+e_a=2e_a+e_b\\)</span>.</p><p>\u201cFree\u201d means that we impose no extra identifications beyond the\nrules of a vector space. Distinct letters give independent vectors:\nwe do not, for example, declare <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(e_c=e_a+e_b\\)</span>.</p><p>A vector space freely generated by a set <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(S\\)</span> has one basis\nvector <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(e_s\\)</span> for each element <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(s\\in S\\)</span>. Its vectors are\nfinite linear combinations of these basis vectors, with coefficients\nin a field <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(K\\)</span>, and each such expression is unique. For example,\na set with three elements generates a copy of <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(K^3\\)</span>, with basis\n<span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(e_1,e_2,e_3\\)</span>. Taking <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(K=\\mathbb{R}\\)</span>, one vector is</p><p><span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(v = 2e_1 - 3e_2 + 0e_3\\)</span>.</p><p>Its coefficients are <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\((2,-3,0)\\)</span>: no different choice of\ncoefficients gives the same vector. If the basis is infinite,\neach vector still uses only finitely many nonzero coefficients.\nNo relations between the basis vectors are imposed.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#term-isomorphism\"]": "<dt id=\"term-isomorphism\">isomorphism</dt><dd><p>A map that preserves the relevant mathematical structure and has an\ninverse that also preserves it. Objects related by an isomorphism\nhave the same structure, though their elements may be represented\ndifferently. For vector spaces, an isomorphism is an invertible\nlinear map. For example, <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\((a,b)\\mapsto(b,a)\\)</span> is an isomorphism\nfrom <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(K^2\\)</span> to itself.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#term-dendrogram\"]": "<dt id=\"term-dendrogram\">dendrogram</dt><dd><p>A hierarchy of nested clusters indexed by a scale. At each scale,\nthe clusters form a partition of the observations; as the scale\nincreases, clusters may merge but never split. It is commonly drawn\nas a tree whose leaves are observations and whose branch heights\nindicate when clusters merge.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#term-injective-map\"]": "<dt id=\"term-injective-map\">injective map</dt><dd><p>A map is injective (or one-to-one) if distinct elements of its source\nhave distinct images. Equivalently, <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(f(x)=f(y)\\)</span> implies\n<span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(x=y\\)</span>. Each element of the target has at most one preimage;\nsome target elements may have none. For example,\n<span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(f:K\\to K^2\\)</span>, <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(f(a)=(a,0)\\)</span>, is injective, but is not\nsurjective because it never reaches a pair with a nonzero second\ncoordinate.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#term-kernel-of-a-linear-map\"]": "<dt id=\"term-kernel-of-a-linear-map\">kernel of a linear map</dt><dd><p>The vectors that a linear map sends to zero. For\n<span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(f:V\\to W\\)</span>, the kernel is\n<span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(\\ker f=\\{v\\in V\\mid f(v)=0\\}\\)</span> and is a vector subspace of\n<span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(V\\)</span>. For example, the kernel of <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(f(a,b)=a+b\\)</span> consists\nof all pairs <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\((a,-a)\\)</span>. A linear map is <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"#term-injective-map\"><span class=\"xref std std-term\">injective</span></a>\nexactly when its kernel contains only the zero vector.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#term-simplicial-complex\"]": "<dt id=\"term-simplicial-complex\">simplicial complex</dt><dd><p>An abstract simplicial complex is a collection of finite nonempty\nsets, called simplices, that contains every nonempty subset of each\nsimplex. The elements of these sets are called vertices. A simplex\nwith <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(k+1\\)</span> vertices has dimension <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(k\\)</span>: a singleton is a\nvertex, a pair is an edge, and a triple is a filled triangle.\nLarger sets describe higher-dimensional analogues: four vertices\nform a filled tetrahedron, and so on.</p><p>For example, including <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(\\{a,b,c\\}\\)</span> requires including its\nthree edges and three vertices. The three edges can also occur\nwithout the filled triangle, giving just its boundary. \u201cAbstract\u201d\nmeans the complex records which vertices form simplices; no\ncoordinates or embedding in Euclidean space are required. Some\nconventions also include the empty set as a simplex.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#term-surjective-map\"]": "<dt id=\"term-surjective-map\">surjective map</dt><dd><p>A map is surjective (or onto) if every element of its target is the\nimage of at least one element of its source. Several source elements\nmay have the same image. For example,\n<span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(f:K^2\\to K\\)</span>, <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(f(a,b)=a+b\\)</span>, is surjective because every\n<span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(c\\in K\\)</span> is the image of <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\((c,0)\\)</span>.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#term-principal-submatrix\"]": "<dt id=\"term-principal-submatrix\">principal submatrix</dt><dd><p>A matrix obtained from a square matrix by keeping the same selection\nof rows and columns. For an index set <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(S\\)</span>, it is written\n<span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(M[S,S]\\)</span>. For example, keeping the first and third rows and\ncolumns of a <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(3\\times 3\\)</span> matrix gives</p><p><span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(\\begin{pmatrix} m_{11} &amp; m_{13} \\\\ m_{31} &amp; m_{33} \\end{pmatrix}\\)</span>.</p><p>For a distance matrix, this retains all pairwise distances between\nthe selected points.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#term-basis-of-a-vector-space\"]": "<dt id=\"term-basis-of-a-vector-space\">basis of a vector space</dt><dd><p>A collection of vectors such that every vector in the space can be\nwritten in exactly one way as a finite linear combination of them.\nFor example, <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\((1,0,0)\\)</span>, <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\((0,1,0)\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\((0,0,1)\\)</span>\nform a basis of <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(K^3\\)</span>: the coefficients in the combination\nare simply the three coordinates. The number of basis vectors is\nthe dimension of the vector space.</p></dd>"}
skip_classes = ["headerlink", "sd-stretched-link"]

window.onload = function () {
    for (const [select, tip_html] of Object.entries(selector_to_html)) {
        const links = document.querySelectorAll(`article.bd-article ${select}`);
        for (const link of links) {
            if (skip_classes.some(c => link.classList.contains(c))) {
                continue;
            }

            tippy(link, {
                content: tip_html,
                allowHTML: true,
                arrow: true,
                placement: 'auto-start', maxWidth: 420, interactive: true, theme: 'glossary',
                onShow(instance) {MathJax.typesetPromise([instance.popper]).then(() => {});},
            });
        };
    };
    console.log("tippy tips loaded!");
};
